WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.870
When N is twenty or smaller, represent any subset of items as an N-bit integer mask.

00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:12.105
Bit i is one if item i belongs to the subset and zero otherwise.

00:00:12.105 --> 00:00:20.120
Bitwise operations test membership, add elements, and remove elements in a single CPU instruction.

00:00:20.433 --> 00:00:31.330
Because setting a new bit always produces a strictly larger integer, iterating masks from zero up to two to the N minus one is naturally topological.

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:38.351
From a valid state, transition to each unset bit using bitwise OR with one shifted left by next.

00:00:38.351 --> 00:00:42.153
Flatten 2D tables into 1D lists when speed is tight.

00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:52.319
Checking all permutations of twenty items takes over two quintillion steps, whereas bitmask DP uses only twenty million operations.

00:00:52.319 --> 00:00:58.561
Only the set of visited items and possibly the last item matter for future choices.

00:00:58.561 --> 00:01:04.427
Compressing ordering history into a subset mask makes NP-hard tours tractable.

00:01:04.733 --> 00:01:16.797
To iterate over all submasks of a mask m, repeatedly set sub to sub minus one bitwise-and m; across all masks this takes O(3 to the N) total steps.

00:01:16.797 --> 00:01:27.384
When summing over all subsets for every mask, Sum Over Subsets DP propagates one bit position at a time in O(N times 2 to the N).
